#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087437 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddb59990-7419-585f-aa1b-901d59c8cfc1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Zero Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "9c82456c-49ea-5a5e-b0a2-0ab17059acee" :question/math-academy-id "e-4390" :question/problem "Which of the following are zero matrices?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "- $P$ is a zero matrix since *all* its entries are zeros.\n- $Q$ is *not* a zero matrix since some of its entries are not zeros (for instance, $q_{12}=0.1\\neq0$).\n- $R$ is a zero matrix since *all* its entries are zeros.\n\nTherefore, only $P$ and $R$ are zero matrices."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087438 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ca13445-0218-5950-967e-a68bcfec32c7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Square Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "bcb710d0-a687-5686-ae3a-0d0773857530" :question/math-academy-id "e-4391" :question/problem "Which of the following are square matrices?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "- $U$ is *not* a square matrix since the numbers of rows and columns are not the same. It has $2$ rows and $3$ columns, so it's a $2 \\times 3$ matrix.\n- $W$ is a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. It has $3$ rows and $3$ columns, so it is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix.\n- $Z$ is a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. It has $2$ rows and $2$ columns, so it is a $2 \\times 2$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, $W$ and $Z$ are square matrices."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087439 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "09fa0899-a3d2-584f-8cdd-a471f8cfd16b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Special Square Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "79974103-2da9-599a-b33b-abac3d32613a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4392" :question/problem "Which of the following are diagonal matrices?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "- $Q$ is a diagonal matrix. It's a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. Also, all the entries that do not lie on the main diagonal are zeros.\n\n> Notice that $Q$ is not an identity matrix since the diagonal entries are not all ones.\n\n- $R$ is *not* a diagonal matrix. It's a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. But some of the entries that do not lie on the main diagonal are not zeros (for instance, $r_{13}=1 \\neq 0$).\n- $S$ is a diagonal matrix. It's a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. Also, all the entries that do not lie on the main diagonal are zeros.\n\n> Notice that $S$ is not an identity matrix since the diagonal entries are not all ones.\n\nTherefore, only $Q$ and $S$ are diagonal matrices."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087440 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b6ddadb-471a-5195-a0f5-fd44bdc8f780" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Other Matrices"}] :question/id #uuid "0e3ad3cd-f7c4-55f4-8a0b-b850283bc6b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4393" :question/problem "How can we best describe the following matrix?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $F$ is a matrix that has a single column. Therefore, it is a column vector."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087923 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ca13445-0218-5950-967e-a68bcfec32c7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Square Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "918fc666-8fd2-5ed5-ab56-1295526c3c1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084550} {:answer/id #uuid "99c24d9f-f749-545f-9c79-396d2c62191b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ and $E$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084551} {:answer/id #uuid "c3668bc6-1489-5dd1-ae20-cb4970245bba" :answer/type {:db